.SH "JOUET AUDIT"
Report where coding\-agent sessions spend tokens and time

Reads Claude Code's transcripts (~/.claude/projects) and Codex's rollouts (~/.codex/sessions and archived_sessions) on this machine, and ranks what their requests spent input tokens on: CI polling, process polling, PR threads, PR status, edits, builds and tests, reads and searches, git and more. For each costly category it names the jouet command or rule that addresses it, with an estimate of the saving, and it counts recurring patterns such as sleep loops. Nothing leaves the machine. Commands are shown only as shapes, with every value replaced, unless \-\-examples asks for redacted examples.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet audit [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-since\fR \fI<TIME>\fR
Only requests made since TIME: a duration ago such as 7d or 12h, a date such as 2026\-10\-01, or RFC 3339
.TP
\fB\-\-harness\fR \fI<HARNESS>\fR
Which transcripts to read: claude, codex or all (default all)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Only sessions that ran in PATH or a directory below it
.TP
\fB\-\-top\fR \fI<N>\fR
Categories to list per harness in text (default 10; 0 lists all). \-\-json lists every category
.TP
\fB\-\-examples\fR
Show one redacted example command per shape and pattern
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET CLAIM"
Claim paths for this session, so other sessions are warned off them

A claim is advisory: it blocks nothing. It tells other agent sessions on this machine that this session works on these paths, and jouet's hooks warn a session that edits or commits a path another live session claims, naming that session and its worktree. Every worktree of a repository shares its claims. PATH is a file, a directory (covering everything under it) or a glob (`*`, `**`, `?`). A claim lasts \-\-for (default 2h), and lapses early when its worktree is removed or its agent exits. Claiming a path again renews it. Refuses (exit 1) when another live session claims an overlapping path, naming it; exits 4 outside an agent session (set JOUET_SESSION_ID to claim from elsewhere).
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet claim [OPTIONS] [<PATHS>] ... [COMMAND]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-for\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
How long the claim lasts (default 2h)
.TP
\fB\-\-note\fR \fI<TEXT>\fR
What the session is doing there, shown to the others
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PATHS>\fR
The files, directories or globs to claim
.SH "JOUET CLAIM LS"
List the live claims in this repository, every session's

Claims that expired, or whose worktree or agent is gone, are removed first; \-\-all lists them before they go.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet claim ls [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-all\fR
Also list the claims that no longer hold
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET CLAIM RELEASE"
Release this session's claims

Releases the claims on the paths given, or all of this session's claims in this repository. Another session's claim is never released: it lapses on its own.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet claim release [OPTIONS] [<PATHS>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PATHS>\fR
The claimed paths to release; all of this session's when none
.SH "JOUET COMMIT"
Commit only the named paths, with trailers

Commits exactly the paths given, as they are in the working tree (`git commit \-\-only`): anything else already staged stays staged, and untracked files are added only when a named path covers them. Refuses \-A, `.`, the repository root and a path with no changes. Paths are literal, never globs. Appends the configured trailers; inside an agent the default is a co\-author line (Claude Code) and the AI disclosure, with the tool and version from AI_AGENT. Warns about a subject that is not a conventional commit when the repository checks them. Hooks always run; when one fails, the end of its output is shown and the whole of it kept in a log.

Before committing, the lines the commit adds are scanned with gitleaks' rules (as `jouet secrets scan \-\-staged` does), and in a project that uses fnox, `fnox scan \-\-values` checks them for its secrets' values. A secret found refuses the commit (exit 1), naming the rule or secret and the file and line, never the value. A false positive is let through with \-\-allow\-secret RULE:PATH:LINE, `gitleaks:allow` on its line, or its fingerprint in .gitleaksignore.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet commit [OPTIONS] [<PATHS>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-m, \-\-message\fR \fI<MSG>\fR
The message. Repeatable: each is a paragraph, as with git
.TP
\fB\-F, \-\-file\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
Read the message from FILE (`\-` is stdin)
.TP
\fB\-\-trailer\fR \fI<TRAILER>\fR
Append a trailer, `Key: value`. Repeatable
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-trailers\fR
Leave out the configured and default trailers
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be committed, committing nothing
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-secret\fR \fI<RULE:PATH:LINE>\fR
Commit this finding of the secret scan anyway, a false positive: RULE:PATH:LINE as the refusal lists it. Repeatable
.TP
\fB\-A, \-\-all\fR
Refused: name the paths to commit instead
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-verify\fR
Refused: jouet commit always runs hooks
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PATHS>\fR
The files and directories to commit
.SH "JOUET SECRETS"
Find secrets in files, in what is staged, or in commits, with gitleaks' rules
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet secrets [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET SECRETS SCAN"
Find API keys, tokens and private keys, with gitleaks' rules

Uses gitleaks' detection rules (github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks, MIT), built in: each rule's keywords, regex, entropy and allowlists, and gitleaks' own allowlists, which leave out lockfiles, images and vendored code. A repository's .gitleaks.toml adds rules and allowlists ([extend] useDefault = true, or allowlists alone) or replaces the rules. A line with `gitleaks:allow` on it, and a finding whose fingerprint (PATH:RULE:LINE) is in .gitleaksignore, are passed over.

Without \-\-staged or \-\-diff, scans the files and directories given (the current directory by default) as they are on disk; directories are walked as git sees them, so ignored files are left out unless named. \-\-staged scans only the lines the index adds to HEAD, which is what `git commit` would record. \-\-diff REF scans the lines each commit in REF..HEAD adds, commit by commit, so a secret added and removed again is still found; REF may also be a range, A..B. With either, the paths limit the diff.

Each finding names its rule, file and line, with the match redacted to at most the secret's first four characters; the secret itself is never printed. Exits 1 when anything is found, 3 for a path or REF that does not exist, and 4 outside a git repository with \-\-staged or \-\-diff, or for a .gitleaks.toml that does not parse.

Examples:
  jouet secrets scan
  jouet secrets scan \-\-staged
  jouet secrets scan \-\-diff origin/main
  jouet secrets scan config/ .env.example
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet secrets scan [OPTIONS] [<PATHS>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-staged\fR
Scan the lines the index adds to HEAD: what `git commit` would record
.TP
\fB\-\-diff\fR \fI<REF>\fR
Scan the lines each commit in REF..HEAD adds, or in a range A..B
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PATHS>\fR
Files and directories to scan; with \-\-staged or \-\-diff, the paths the diff is limited to
.SH "JOUET DIAG"
Report the errors and warnings in files you changed, through hk

With an hk config (hk.pkl), runs `hk run check \-\-safe \-\-format json` on exactly the files given, or with \-\-changed (the default) those that differ from HEAD or are untracked, and prints hk's normalised findings: file, line, column, severity, rule, message and the step that found it. A step that sets no `diagnostic_format` is reported with its status and the end of its output. Without an hk config, falls back to cargo clippy, ruff or shellcheck in their JSON modes, or says diagnostics are unavailable. Exits 0 when clean, 1 with findings, 4 when hk refused to run (`\-\-safe` rejects commands whose effect is unknown or destructive) or nothing can check the files.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet diag [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET DIAG CHECK"
Check files and report every finding

Each check also remembers, per file, what it found in that version of the file, which is what `jouet diag delta` compares with.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet diag check [OPTIONS] [<FILES>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-changed\fR
The files that differ from HEAD or are untracked
.TP
\fB\-\-max\fR \fI<N>\fR
Show at most N findings (default 50); the rest are counted
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<FILES>\fR
Files to check (default: \-\-changed)
.SH "JOUET DIAG DELTA"
Report only the findings an edit introduced

Checks the files, then compares each with the last check of an earlier version of it: a finding is the same one when its file, rule and step match and its message matches or it is within three lines, so findings that only moved are not new. A file unchanged since HEAD has nothing new. A changed file no check has seen before has no baseline; its findings are listed apart and not counted. Exits 1 when the edit introduced a finding or made a step without diagnostics fail.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet diag delta [OPTIONS] [<FILES>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-changed\fR
The files that differ from HEAD or are untracked
.TP
\fB\-\-max\fR \fI<N>\fR
Show at most N findings (default 50); the rest are counted
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<FILES>\fR
Files to check (default: \-\-changed)
.SH "JOUET DIAG FIX"
Format files with the project's fixers (`hk run fix \-\-safe`)

Runs hk's fix steps on exactly these files, refusing (exit 4) a config with a fixer whose effect is unknown or destructive, and reports which files changed. Needs an hk config.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet diag fix [OPTIONS] [<FILES>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-changed\fR
The files that differ from HEAD or are untracked
.TP
\fB\-\-step\fR \fI<STEP>\fR
Only this hk step. Repeatable
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Say what would run, changing nothing
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<FILES>\fR
Files to format (default: \-\-changed)
.SH "JOUET EDIT"
Make exact, all\-or\-nothing edits across files

Reads a spec from \-\-spec or stdin: search/replace blocks, a Codex\-style patch, or JSON. Every edit is checked before any file is written, so a failed edit leaves every file as it was.

A SEARCH block holds whole lines, indentation included: one that holds part of a line matches nothing, and the failure says which line the text is inside. `jouet edit replace` matches text anywhere, inside a line too.

A blocks section headed `=== PATH#SYMBOL` replaces a whole definition, found with tree\-sitter, so a formatter reflowing its text cannot break the match. SYMBOL is a name, a kind and a name (`fn download`), or a qualified name (`Vfox::download`), as `jouet read FILE \-\-outline` lists them; it must match exactly one definition. The new text runs to the next `===` line, at any indentation: it is indented to where the definition sits. Comments and attributes above the definition stay. An edit that would add syntax errors is refused. When an edit changes the definition's first line, its callers in other files are listed from the code graph (`jouet graph`), or the change is noted when there is none.

  === src/backend/vfox.rs#Vfox::download
  pub fn download(&self, url: &str, retries: u32) \-> Result<()> {
      ...
  }

`\-\-pattern PAT \-\-rewrite REPL \-\-lang LANG [PATH...]` rewrites every match of a structural pattern (ast\-grep's) under the paths, instead of reading a spec. The rewrites go through the same all\-or\-nothing writes, \-\-dry\-run, \-\-diff, \-\-check and \-\-revert\-on\-fail, a rewrite that adds syntax errors is refused, and a pattern that matches nothing writes nothing (exit 4). A pattern is code in LANG:

\- Quote it in single quotes. In double quotes the shell expands `$X`,
  and `"$X.unwrap()"` becomes `.unwrap()`, which matches nothing.
\- `$X` (uppercase) matches exactly one node; `$$$X` matches any number,
  such as all of a call's arguments. `foo($A)` matches only one\-argument
  calls, `foo($$$ARGS)` every call. `$_` matches without capturing.
\- It must be whole code: `client.get($URL)`, not `get($URL`. A Rust
  call pattern matches the call, not a `?` or `;` after it.
\- A name used twice must match the same text twice: `$A == $A`. \- REPL is text with the pattern's `$X` filled in; the rest is copied as
  written, and indentation follows the match.

`\-\-callers\-of PATH#SYMBOL` rewrites only the calls the code graph attributes to that definition, so `load($$$A)` leaves other functions named `load` alone; write the pattern for the whole call (`$F($P)`, `$R.load($$$A)`). `\-\-impact PATH#SYMBOL` rewrites only inside it and the code depending on it. Matches the graph cannot attribute (dynamic dispatch, macros, files it has not read) are listed as unresolved and left alone, and so are its calls the pattern missed. The report lists each rewrite and the test files that depend on them; \-\-dry\-run first.

Examples:
  jouet edit \-\-pattern '$C.get($URL).send()?' \-\-rewrite '$C.get($URL).timeout(TIMEOUT).send()?' \-\-lang rust src/
  jouet edit \-\-pattern 'print($$$ARGS)' \-\-rewrite 'log.info($$$ARGS)' \-\-lang python app/ \-\-check 'python \-m pytest \-q' \-\-revert\-on\-fail
  jouet edit \-\-dry\-run \-\-pattern '$F($P)' \-\-rewrite '$F($P, None)' \-\-lang rust \-\-callers\-of src/config.rs#load

Try a pattern with `jouet search \-\-structural \-\-lang LANG 'PAT'` first.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet edit [OPTIONS] [<PATHS>] ... [COMMAND]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-spec\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
Read the spec from FILE instead of stdin (`\-` is stdin)
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fI<FORMAT>\fR
Spec format: blocks, patch or json. Detected from the first line
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Report what would change without writing anything
.TP
\fB\-\-diff\fR
Include a unified diff of each changed file
.TP
\fB\-\-check\fR \fI<CMD>\fR
Run CMD through the shell after writing
.TP
\fB\-\-diag\fR
Fail if the edit introduced diagnostics: the edited files are checked through hk (`jouet diag`) before and after writing, and only new findings count
.TP
\fB\-\-revert\-on\-fail\fR
Restore every edited file if \-\-check or \-\-diag fails
.TP
\fB\-\-why\fR
Warn when the edit changes lines an earlier agent session wrote, by `entire why`: the session, checkpoint and prompt behind them, so `\-\-dry\-run \-\-why` shows it before anything is written. It warns and does not refuse. Needs Entire enabled in the repository; off by default because `entire why` can take a minute where the history is long. Looks at the first 10 files
.TP
\fB\-\-pattern\fR \fI<PAT>\fR
Rewrite code matching this ast\-grep pattern, instead of reading a spec
.TP
\fB\-\-rewrite\fR \fI<REPL>\fR
What \-\-pattern's matches become; its `$X` are the pattern's
.TP
\fB\-\-lang\fR \fI<LANG>\fR
The language of \-\-pattern: rust, python, javascript, typescript, tsx, go, ruby, java, c or cpp
.TP
\fB\-\-callers\-of\fR \fI<PATH#SYMBOL>\fR
With \-\-pattern, rewrite only the calls the code graph attributes to this symbol, given as PATH#NAME (or #ID from `jouet graph symbols`)
.TP
\fB\-\-impact\fR \fI<PATH#SYMBOL>\fR
With \-\-pattern, rewrite only inside this symbol and the code the graph says depends on it, three hops back
.TP
\fB\-\-expect\-hash\fR \fI<PATH=SHA256>\fR
Refuse unless PATH's contents have this SHA\-256 hash. Repeatable
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PATHS>\fR
With \-\-pattern, the files and directories to rewrite (default .)
.SH "JOUET EDIT REPLACE"
Replace text in files, given on the command line

Matches anywhere in a file, like `str::replace`, and requires exactly one match in each file unless \-\-count or \-\-all says otherwise. Repeat \-\-old and \-\-new for several replacements: the Nth \-\-new replaces the Nth \-\-old, in order. Every replacement applies to every file, and every file is checked before any is written, so a rename across files is one call that lands whole or not at all.

Examples:
  jouet edit replace src/lib.rs \-\-old 'let x = 1;' \-\-new 'let x = 2;'
  jouet edit replace a.py b.py \-\-old retry_limit \-\-new max_attempts \-\-all
  jouet edit replace app.py \-\-old 'a()' \-\-new 'b()' \-\-old 'c()' \-\-new 'd()'
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet edit replace [OPTIONS] [<PATHS>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-old\fR \fI<TEXT>\fR
The text to find. Repeatable
.TP
\fB\-\-new\fR \fI<TEXT>\fR
The text to put in its place, one for each \-\-old
.TP
\fB\-\-count\fR \fI<N>\fR
How many matches each \-\-old must have in each file
.TP
\fB\-\-all\fR
Replace every match (at least one in each file)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PATHS>\fR
The files to edit
.SH "JOUET EDIT HASH"
Print the SHA\-256 hash of files, for \-\-expect\-hash
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet edit hash [OPTIONS] [<PATHS>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PATHS>\fR
Files to hash
.SH "JOUET GRAPH"
Query the code graph: symbols, callers, impact and affected tests

The graph is built with tree\-sitter from Rust, Python, TypeScript and JavaScript, Go, Ruby, Java, C and C++ files, and kept in a database in $XDG_CACHE_HOME/jouet/graph, one per work tree: nothing is written into the repository. There is no daemon: every query first re\-indexes the files that changed since the last one, and jouet's post\-tool\-use hook re\-indexes files an agent edits. The code graph builds on codegraph's design and extraction.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet graph [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET GRAPH INDEX"
Build the graph, or bring it up to date

Lists the work tree's source files (honouring .gitignore), and extracts only the files whose content changed since the last index. Files larger than 1 MiB or not UTF\-8 are recorded without symbols. A worktree with no graph yet starts from a copy of the graph of a sibling worktree of the same repository, the one whose HEAD is nearest, so only the files that differ are extracted.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet graph index [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-full\fR
Drop the graph and index everything from scratch
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET GRAPH STATUS"
Show where the graph lives and what it holds

Prints the database's path and size, and how many files, symbols, edges and references it holds. Does not index.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet graph status [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET GRAPH SYMBOLS"
Find symbols by name, qualified name or words

An exact name comes first, then a qualified name (`Client::push`, `Client.push`), then symbols whose name, docstring or signature has every word (`retry backoff`; `processPayment` has the words `process` and `payment`). With no QUERY, lists every symbol under \-\-path. Each line is `path:line kind qualified\-name`, and the ID after `#` names the symbol to other graph commands.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet graph symbols [OPTIONS] [<QUERY>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-kind\fR \fI<KIND>\fR
Only symbols of this kind: function, method, class, struct, …
.TP
\fB\-\-path\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Only symbols in this file or directory
.TP
\fB\-\-budget\fR \fI<BYTES>\fR
The most bytes to print (default 6000; 0 is no limit)
.TP
\fB\-\-page\fR \fI<N>\fR
Which page of a longer answer to print (default 1)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<QUERY>\fR
A name, a qualified name, or words
.SH "JOUET GRAPH CALLERS"
A symbol's callers, callees or impact

SYMBOL is a name, a qualified name (`Client::push`), `PATH#NAME`, `PATH:LINE` (the innermost symbol there) or an ID from `jouet graph symbols`. A name that several symbols share answers for each of them. callers and callees follow calls, constructions and references; impact follows every edge but containment, from the symbol and everything it contains, and groups the answer by file.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet graph callers [OPTIONS] <SYMBOL>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-depth\fR \fI<N>\fR
How many hops to follow (default 1, and 3 for impact)
.TP
\fB\-\-budget\fR \fI<BYTES>\fR
The most bytes to print (default 6000; 0 is no limit)
.TP
\fB\-\-page\fR \fI<N>\fR
Which page of a longer answer to print (default 1)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<SYMBOL>\fR
The symbol
.SH "JOUET GRAPH CALLEES"
A symbol's callers, callees or impact

SYMBOL is a name, a qualified name (`Client::push`), `PATH#NAME`, `PATH:LINE` (the innermost symbol there) or an ID from `jouet graph symbols`. A name that several symbols share answers for each of them. callers and callees follow calls, constructions and references; impact follows every edge but containment, from the symbol and everything it contains, and groups the answer by file.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet graph callees [OPTIONS] <SYMBOL>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-depth\fR \fI<N>\fR
How many hops to follow (default 1, and 3 for impact)
.TP
\fB\-\-budget\fR \fI<BYTES>\fR
The most bytes to print (default 6000; 0 is no limit)
.TP
\fB\-\-page\fR \fI<N>\fR
Which page of a longer answer to print (default 1)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<SYMBOL>\fR
The symbol
.SH "JOUET GRAPH IMPACT"
A symbol's callers, callees or impact

SYMBOL is a name, a qualified name (`Client::push`), `PATH#NAME`, `PATH:LINE` (the innermost symbol there) or an ID from `jouet graph symbols`. A name that several symbols share answers for each of them. callers and callees follow calls, constructions and references; impact follows every edge but containment, from the symbol and everything it contains, and groups the answer by file.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet graph impact [OPTIONS] <SYMBOL>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-depth\fR \fI<N>\fR
How many hops to follow (default 1, and 3 for impact)
.TP
\fB\-\-budget\fR \fI<BYTES>\fR
The most bytes to print (default 6000; 0 is no limit)
.TP
\fB\-\-page\fR \fI<N>\fR
Which page of a longer answer to print (default 1)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<SYMBOL>\fR
The symbol
.SH "JOUET GRAPH EXPLORE"
Find the code that answers a question, with its source and neighbours

Ranks symbols by how well their names (split into words), qualified names, docstrings and signatures match the question, puts tests after the code they test unless the question is about tests, and prints each with its signature, docstring, callers, callees and the start of its source. The answer is paged rather than printed whole: a dense answer stays in the agent's context for the rest of the session.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet graph explore [OPTIONS] [<QUERY>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-budget\fR \fI<BYTES>\fR
The most bytes to print (default 6000; 0 is no limit)
.TP
\fB\-\-source\-budget\fR \fI<BYTES>\fR
The most bytes of one symbol's source (default 1200; 0 for none)
.TP
\fB\-\-page\fR \fI<N>\fR
Which page of a longer answer to print (default 1)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<QUERY>\fR
The question, or the names you know (`retry backoff for webhooks`)
.SH "JOUET GRAPH AFFECTED"
List the test files that depend on changed files

Follows the graph backwards from each FILE, through imports, calls, references and inheritance, to every test file that reaches it, and shows the chain that does. A FILE the graph cannot speak for (not indexed, or in a language jouet does not extract) is listed as unknown, and the exit code is 1: run the whole suite. A fallback for languages without a native tool; `jouet test \-\-affected` prefers the native one.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet graph affected [OPTIONS] [<FILES>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<FILES>\fR
The changed files
.SH "JOUET GRAPH PRUNE"
Delete the graphs of worktrees that no longer exist

Each worktree's graph records its root. This deletes every graph in $XDG_CACHE_HOME/jouet/graph whose root is gone, and keeps one whose root exists or cannot be checked. Querying a graph also does this, at most once a day.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet graph prune [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET HANDOFF"
Record the facts about this checkout for the next session

Gathers the branch, its upstream and ahead/behind counts, HEAD, an operation in progress, uncommitted and untracked files, the branch's PR and its `pr status` attention codes, the session's last test, lint and build runs (recorded by `jouet setup`'s hooks), background processes running in the worktree (Linux), and its `jouet job`s. Writes them as markdown with the data embedded, to `<git dir>/jouet/handoff.md` unless \-o names a file (`.json` writes JSON; `\-` is stdout). Facts only: what the work is and what to do next is the agent's to add.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet handoff [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-o, \-\-output\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
Write the handoff to FILE (`\-` is stdout)
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-pr\fR
Do not look up the branch's PR on GitHub
.TP
\fB\-\-session\fR \fI<ID>\fR
The agent session whose check runs to include (default JOUET_SESSION_ID, else CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET PICKUP"
Print a handoff and check that it still holds

Reads `<git dir>/jouet/handoff.md`, or FILE, prints it, and compares it with the checkout: the branch, HEAD, the upstream, an operation in progress, uncommitted and untracked files, and whether its background processes still run. Exits 0 when nothing has drifted and 1 when something has. The PR is not fetched again: run `jouet pr status` for its current state. Where the repository has Entire (`entire` on PATH, the repository enabled, a working login), it also lists the earlier sessions with checkpoints on this branch: the last checkpoint of each and the files it touched, and the `entire` commands that show more.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet pickup [OPTIONS] [<FILE>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<FILE>\fR
The handoff to read (`\-` is stdin)
.SH "JOUET SESSIONS"
Show which agent sessions are working where on this machine

Reads Claude Code's transcripts (~/.claude/projects, or $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR) and Codex's rollouts (~/.codex/sessions, or $CODEX_HOME) written within \-\-since (default 24h). For each session it shows the harness, the directory it last worked in, that directory's work tree and checked\-out branch, and when its transcript was last written. A session is live when its transcript was written in the last 15 minutes. Only the end of each transcript is read, never what the session said, and nothing is written. Warns about two live sessions in one work tree, which commit each other's uncommitted work, and about a live session whose directory is gone. Lists live sessions; \-\-all also lists idle ones, marking those whose directory is gone. Where the repository in the current directory has Entire, the sessions Entire tracks there are added: a session jouet found gets its checkpoint count, and one only Entire knows (another agent, or a transcript kept elsewhere) is listed with it. Exits 0 whether or not there are warnings.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet sessions [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-since\fR \fI<TIME>\fR
Only sessions active since TIME: a duration ago such as 24h or 7d, a date such as 2026\-10\-01, or RFC 3339 (default 24h)
.TP
\fB\-\-all\fR
List idle sessions too, not only live ones
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET STATS"
Show what jouet saved: measured from its ledger, estimated from your history

Counts what the hook ledger recorded (jouet command runs, rule decisions and what the agent did after a denial, checks, and failing CI logs returned as excerpts), then estimates the input tokens those runs saved against your own Claude Code and Codex history from before `jouet setup`. Every estimate shows its basis, and a kind of work with fewer than 20 runs before setup is not estimated. Without a ledger, it shows what one run of each command would save.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet stats [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-since\fR \fI<TIME>\fR
Only ledger entries since TIME: a duration ago such as 7d or 12h, a date such as 2026\-10\-01, or RFC 3339
.TP
\fB\-\-harness\fR \fI<HARNESS>\fR
Which harness: claude, codex or all (default all)
.TP
\fB\-\-session\fR \fI<ID>\fR
Only one session's ledger entries
.TP
\fB\-\-summary\fR
Print one line about what jouet saved, or nothing, for a stop hook. `[stats] summary` in the config file or JOUET_STATS_SUMMARY sets its style: quips (default), plain or off
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET PR"
Inspect pull requests
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet pr [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET PR STATUS"
Show where PRs stand and why any need attention

Prints one line per PR, then a summary. GitHub PRs are fetched in one batched GraphQL query; GitLab merge requests and Forgejo or Gitea PRs (named HOST/OWNER/NAME#N, by URL, or through a remote on that forge) are read through their REST APIs. Each PR lists fixed attention codes: conflict, checks_failing, checks_missing, changes_requested, unresolved_threads, entire_findings, entire_gates, behind_base, draft and mergeable_unknown.

A PR with an Entire Gates check gets a line saying which gate fails and why. The check is not counted as a failing check of its own: a failed checks gate repeats the checks above, an up\-to\-date gate is behind_base, and a findings gate is entire_findings, naming the open findings when the `entire` CLI is on PATH and logged in. Without it, `\-\-json` says why under `entire`.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet pr status [OPTIONS] [<PRS>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-mine\fR
Add the open PRs authored by the token's user
.TP
\fB\-\-search\fR \fI<QUERY>\fR
Add the PRs matching a GitHub search query
.TP
\fB\-\-repo\fR \fI<OWNER/NAME>\fR
The repository for bare numbers, branches, \-\-mine and \-\-search. Repeatable. Defaults to the git remote
.TP
\fB\-\-settle\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
How long to wait for GitHub to compute an unknown mergeable state (default 20s; 0 disables)
.TP
\fB\-\-fail\-on\-attention\fR
Exit 1 when any PR needs attention
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PRS>\fR
PRs as numbers, URLs, OWNER/REPO#N or branch names. Defaults to the current branch's PR
.SH "JOUET PR NEXT"
Say the one thing each PR needs next, and the command for it

Works through a fixed order and stops at the first state that applies: merged, closed, draft, conflict, mergeable_unknown, behind_base, checks_failing, checks_pending, changes_requested, unresolved_threads, entire_findings, entire_gates, awaiting_approval, blocked, auto_merge, ready_to_merge. The states come from the same data and attention codes as `pr status`. Exits 0 when nothing is left to do, 10 when the agent must act and 20 when waiting on someone or something else; with several PRs, 10 wins over 20. These two codes are outside the shared table on purpose.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet pr next [OPTIONS] [<PRS>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-mine\fR
Add the open PRs authored by the token's user
.TP
\fB\-\-search\fR \fI<QUERY>\fR
Add the PRs matching a GitHub search query
.TP
\fB\-\-repo\fR \fI<OWNER/NAME>\fR
The repository for bare numbers, branches, \-\-mine and \-\-search. Repeatable. Defaults to the git remote
.TP
\fB\-\-settle\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
How long to wait for GitHub to compute an unknown mergeable state (default 20s; 0 disables)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PRS>\fR
PRs as numbers, URLs, OWNER/REPO#N or branch names. Defaults to the current branch's PR
.SH "JOUET PR LAND"
Merge a PR that is ready, honouring branch protection

Refuses (exit 4) unless `pr next` says ready_to_merge, and says what is missing. With \-\-update, a branch behind its base is first updated on GitHub by merging the base in (never a force\-push), and its new checks are waited for. The merge goes through the API with the head SHA as a guard, the way the merge button does, so branch protection applies and is never bypassed. Then the PR is confirmed merged and the merge commit printed. It asks first only when a person is at a terminal (stdin and stderr are terminals and no coding agent is detected); anyone else must pass \-\-yes or \-\-dry\-run, or it exits 2 before contacting GitHub.

While jouet's hooks keep a ledger, it also refuses (exit 4) unless they recorded a full test run passing at the PR head's tree; pass \-\-skip\-verify with a reason, which the ledger keeps, to land anyway.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet pr land [OPTIONS] [<PR>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-repo\fR \fI<OWNER/NAME>\fR
The repository for a bare number or branch. Defaults to the git remote
.TP
\fB\-\-method\fR \fI<METHOD>\fR
How to merge: squash, merge or rebase (default squash)
.TP
\fB\-\-update\fR
Update a branch that is behind its base by merging the base in on GitHub, then wait for its checks
.TP
\fB\-\-yes\fR
Merge without asking
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Print the plan and change nothing
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
How long to wait for checks after \-\-update (default 30m)
.TP
\fB\-\-interval\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
Time between the first checks of that wait; backs off to 60s (default 10s)
.TP
\fB\-\-settle\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
How long to wait for GitHub to compute an unknown mergeable state (default 20s; 0 disables)
.TP
\fB\-\-skip\-verify\fR \fI<REASON>\fR
Land without a full test pass recorded at the PR head's tree, and say why; the reason is kept in the hook ledger
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PR>\fR
The PR: a number, URL, OWNER/REPO#N or branch. Defaults to the current branch's PR
.SH "JOUET PR THREADS"
List, reply to and resolve review threads

Lists unresolved threads by default, outdated ones included and marked. Each thread is addressed by its first comment's ID, the number in the web UI's `#discussion_r` links (`#note_` on GitLab, `#issuecomment\-` on Forgejo). Forgejo cannot resolve threads through its API, so resolving there exits 4 before anything is posted. Bodies are condensed: HTML comments and badges are removed, `<details>` collapse to their summary and each comment is cut at 30 lines with a marker; suggestion blocks are always kept whole.

When the `entire` CLI is on PATH and logged in, the open findings on the PR's Entire trail follow the threads, each addressed as `entire:ID`. They are Entire's, not GitHub's: resolving a finding leaves the threads as they were. A stale finding counts as outdated and an agent's as a bot's. Without the CLI, `\-\-json` says why under `entire`.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet pr threads [OPTIONS] [<PR>] [COMMAND]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-repo\fR \fI<OWNER/NAME>\fR
The repository for a bare number or branch. Defaults to the git remote
.TP
\fB\-\-all\fR
List resolved threads too
.TP
\fB\-\-resolved\fR
List only resolved threads
.TP
\fB\-\-outdated\fR
List only outdated threads
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-outdated\fR
Leave out outdated threads
.TP
\fB\-\-author\fR \fI<LOGIN>\fR
Only threads started by LOGIN. Repeatable
.TP
\fB\-\-bots\fR
Only threads started by bots: logins ending in `[bot]`, and the config's `bots`
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-bots\fR
Leave out threads started by bots
.TP
\fB\-\-since\fR \fI<TIME>\fR
Only threads with a comment posted at or after TIME: RFC 3339, a duration ago such as 2h, or @SHA for when that commit was pushed. Goes by when comments were posted, never by the commit they are on
.TP
\fB\-\-path\fR \fI<GLOB>\fR
Only threads on paths matching GLOB, a directory, or (without a /) a file name. Repeatable
.TP
\fB\-\-include\-reviews\fR
Also list review summaries and PR conversation comments
.TP
\fB\-\-context\fR \fI<N>\fR
Show N lines of the file around each thread's line
.TP
\fB\-\-full\fR
Show comment bodies unaltered
.TP
\fB\-\-fail\-if\-unresolved\fR
Exit 1 when any listed thread is unresolved
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PR>\fR
The PR: a number, URL, OWNER/REPO#N or branch. Defaults to the current branch's PR
.SH "JOUET PR THREADS REPLY"
Reply to a thread

`{sha}` in the reply becomes the short HEAD SHA, and is refused unless HEAD is on the PR's branch on GitHub. The configured reply footer is appended. A reply that is already the thread's last comment from you is not posted again.

For an Entire finding (`entire:ID`), the reply is the reason recorded on Entire, so it needs \-\-resolve or \-\-dismiss.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet pr threads reply [OPTIONS] <ID> [<PR>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-body\fR \fI<TEXT>\fR
The reply
.TP
\fB\-\-body\-file\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
Read the reply from FILE (`\-` is stdin)
.TP
\fB\-\-resolve\fR
Resolve the thread after replying
.TP
\fB\-\-dismiss\fR
Dismiss the Entire finding instead of resolving it, with the reply as the reason
.TP
\fB\-\-ignore\-resolved\fR
Leave a thread that is already resolved alone; without this, a reply still goes into it
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Check everything and show what would be posted, posting nothing
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ID>\fR
The thread's ID, or an Entire finding's handle (`entire:ID`)
.TP
\fB<PR>\fR
The PR. Defaults to the current branch's PR
.SH "JOUET PR THREADS RESOLVE"
Resolve threads

Every ID is checked before any thread is resolved. A PR given after the IDs must not be a bare number: write it as #N.

Entire findings (`entire:ID`, as `jouet pr threads` lists them) are resolved on Entire through the `entire` CLI, with \-\-reply as the reason; \-\-dismiss dismisses them instead, and needs a reason. A finding and a GitHub thread are separate: resolving one never resolves the other.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet pr threads resolve [OPTIONS] [<ARGS>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-reply\fR \fI<TEXT>\fR
Reply with TEXT before resolving each thread; for an Entire finding, the reason recorded
.TP
\fB\-\-dismiss\fR
Dismiss the Entire findings instead of resolving them; needs \-\-reply
.TP
\fB\-\-ignore\-resolved\fR
Leave threads that are already resolved alone; without this, replies still go into them
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Check everything and show what would happen, changing nothing
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ARGS>\fR
Thread IDs or Entire finding handles, then optionally the PR
.SH "JOUET PR THREADS UNRESOLVE"
Unresolve threads

A PR given after the IDs must not be a bare number: write it as #N. An Entire finding (`entire:ID`) is reopened on Entire.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet pr threads unresolve [OPTIONS] [<ARGS>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Check everything and show what would happen, changing nothing
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ARGS>\fR
Thread IDs or Entire finding handles, then optionally the PR
.SH "JOUET PR THREADS APPLY"
Reply to and resolve many threads from a JSON list

The list holds {"id": N, "reply": "...", "resolve": true} entries. Every entry is checked before anything is posted. Re\-running the same list after a failure is safe: resolved threads and replies already posted are skipped.

An entry for an Entire finding has its handle as the ID, {"id": "entire:ID", "reply": "...", "resolve": true}, with "dismiss": true to dismiss it (which needs a reply) or "unresolve": true to reopen it. Its reply is the reason recorded on Entire.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet pr threads apply [OPTIONS] [<ARGS>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-file\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
Read the list from FILE (`\-` is stdin)
.TP
\fB\-\-ignore\-resolved\fR
Leave threads that are already resolved alone; without this, replies still go into them
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Check everything and show what would be posted, posting nothing
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ARGS>\fR
`\-` to read the list from stdin, and optionally the PR
.SH "JOUET READ"
Read a file by its outline, a symbol or a range, or the repository's map

Reading a large file whole to find one thing fills the context with code that is not needed. Read it in steps instead:

1. `\-\-outline`: every definition with its line range, as `kind name
   (start\-end)`, the name qualified by its scope (`Vfox::download`).
2. `\-\-symbol NAME`: one definition's code. NAME is a name, a kind and a
   name (`fn download`, `class Cart`), or a qualified name
   (`Vfox::download`, `Shop.Cart`); every definition it matches is printed.
3. `\-\-range A:B`: lines A to B (`A:` to the end, `:B` from the start).

With none of them, a file of up to 300 lines is printed whole, and a longer one as its outline. `\-\-outline` prints a file of up to 100 lines whole too (with its outline in \-\-json), since reading it after its outline would cost another call. Code is printed with line numbers, at most \-\-max\-lines lines (default 400; 0 is no limit) with a marker for the rest. Inside an agent session, lines this session was already shown, from a file unchanged since, collapse to one line; \-\-again prints them. Definitions come from tree\-sitter, for Rust, Python, JavaScript, TypeScript, Go, Ruby, Java, C and C++.

`\-\-map` prints, for the files that matter most, the line of each of their most important definitions, sized to a token budget, so an agent can find its way around a repository without reading whole files. It is Aider's repo map on the code graph (`jouet graph`): files are ranked by personalised PageRank over which file uses which definition, as the graph resolved them, and as many top\-ranked definitions are shown as fit the budget. \-\-focus names a file you are working on, or an identifier you care about: what it uses ranks higher, and a focused file's own definitions are left out, as you already have it open. The budget defaults to 1024 tokens with \-\-focus and 8192 without. With a PATH, only files under that directory are listed.

Exits 3 for a file or symbol that does not exist, and 2 for an outline or symbol of a file whose language jouet cannot read.

Examples:
  jouet read src/backend/vfox.rs \-\-outline
  jouet read src/backend/vfox.rs \-\-symbol 'Vfox::download'
  jouet read src/backend/vfox.rs \-\-range 120:160
  jouet read \-\-map \-\-focus src/backend/vfox.rs \-\-focus download
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet read [OPTIONS] [<PATH>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-outline\fR
List the file's definitions and their line ranges (a file of up to 100 lines is printed whole)
.TP
\fB\-\-symbol\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
Print the definitions this names: NAME, KIND NAME or Scope::NAME
.TP
\fB\-\-range\fR \fI<A:B>\fR
Print lines A to B: `A:B`, `A:` or `:B`
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-lines\fR \fI<N>\fR
The most lines of code to print (default 400; 0 is no limit)
.TP
\fB\-\-again\fR
Print lines this session was already shown, too
.TP
\fB\-\-why\fR
With \-\-symbol or \-\-range, say where the lines came from, by `entire why`: the agent session, checkpoint and prompt that wrote them, or the commits people made them in. Needs Entire enabled in the repository. Off by default because `entire why` takes from tens of milliseconds to two minutes where the history is long ([entire] why_timeout_ms)
.TP
\fB\-\-map\fR
Print a map of the repository's most important definitions, from the code graph
.TP
\fB\-\-budget\fR \fI<TOKENS>\fR
The map's size in tokens, estimated at 4 bytes each
.TP
\fB\-\-focus\fR \fI<FILE|NAME>\fR
For the map: a file you are working on, or an identifier you care about. Repeatable
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PATH>\fR
The file to read, or with \-\-map the directory to map (default .)
.SH "JOUET DOCS"
Look up a dependency's API at the version the project locked

Answers from the sources already installed for the project, at the version its lockfile names, rather than from memory or a hosted index of some other version. PKG's version comes from the nearest lockfile, from the current directory up to the repository's root: Cargo.lock; package\-lock.json, pnpm\-lock.yaml, yarn.lock or bun.lock; uv.lock, poetry.lock or the virtualenv's metadata; go.mod. Its sources are read where the package manager installed them: the cargo registry ($CARGO_HOME) or a vendor directory, node_modules (its .d.ts files, else @types/PKG, else its JavaScript), the virtualenv's site\-packages ($VIRTUAL_ENV, .venv or venv; a .pyi stub before its .py), the Go module cache ($GOMODCACHE) or vendor/. Nothing is downloaded: a package that is locked but not installed exits 4 with the command that installs it.

Without SYMBOL, an outline of the public API: each definition's path, kind and signature with the first sentence of its doc, the package's root first, cut to \-\-budget with a marker naming what was left out. With a module's path as SYMBOL, that module's outline.

With SYMBOL, each definition it names: the version and the file:line it came from, its signature, its doc comment, the start of its source, and for a type its methods, fields or variants (and for a Rust type the traits it derives and implements). SYMBOL is a path from the package's root, with or without its name (`Agent::get`, `ureq::Agent::get`, `requests.Session.get`), else the end of one (`get`). Several matches are each shown, shortest path first, up to three, then listed.

Public means: for Rust, plain `pub` items reachable from the crate root through `pub mod`s and `pub use`s (followed through the crate; one into another crate names it, at the version the lockfile has), without #[doc(hidden)]; for TypeScript, exported declarations; for Python, names without a leading `_`, or those in `__all__`, with re\-exports followed; for Go, capitalised names. \-\-private includes the rest.

The index of each package version is built once, from the code graph's parsers, and cached under jouet's cache directory.

Exits 3 for a package no lockfile names or a symbol it does not define, and 4 for one that is locked but not installed.

Examples:
  jouet docs ureq
  jouet docs ureq Agent::get
  jouet docs reqwest blocking
  jouet docs zod ZodType
  jouet docs click Context.invoke
  jouet docs github.com/spf13/cobra Command.Execute
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet docs [OPTIONS] <PACKAGE> [<SYMBOL>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-lang\fR \fI<LANG>\fR
The ecosystem, when PKG is in more than one lockfile here: rust, ts, python or go
.TP
\fB\-\-budget\fR \fI<TOKENS>\fR
The outline's size in tokens, estimated at 4 bytes each (default 2048)
.TP
\fB\-\-private\fR
Include private definitions too
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PACKAGE>\fR
The package: a crate, an npm package, a Python distribution or import name, or a Go package path; PKG@VERSION picks one of several locked versions
.TP
\fB<SYMBOL>\fR
The definition or module to show (default: the package's outline)
.SH "JOUET DEPS"
Check a package before adding it, or the locked dependencies, against its registry and OSV

Asks the package's registry (crates.io, npm, PyPI or the Go module proxy) and OSV (osv.dev), so a dependency that does not exist, is deprecated or yanked, or has a known advisory is caught before it is added. Only package names and versions are sent; a package the lockfile says comes from a private registry, a path or git is never sent. Answers are cached under jouet's cache directory: a registry's for ten minutes, OSV's for a day.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet deps [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET DEPS INFO"
Say whether a package exists, its latest version, licence, repository and advisories

PKG is ECOSYSTEM:NAME[@VERSION], where ECOSYSTEM is cargo, npm, pypi or go. A bare NAME takes its ecosystem from the lockfile that locks it, or else from the one kind of manifest here (Cargo.toml, package.json, pyproject.toml or requirements.txt, go.mod). The version checked is the one given, else the one the lockfile locks, else the latest.

For a package that does not exist, crates.io's and npm's search name close matches; PyPI and the Go proxy have no search. Go modules have no licence in the proxy.

Exits 3 when a package does not exist (or has no such version), 1 when the version checked has an advisory or is yanked or retracted, or the package is deprecated, 4 when the lockfile locks it from somewhere other than the public registry (its name is not sent), and 5 when a registry or OSV cannot be reached.

Examples:
  jouet deps info cargo:serde_json
  jouet deps info npm:lodash@4.17.15
  jouet deps info pypi:requests go:github.com/spf13/cobra
  jouet deps info zod
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet deps info [OPTIONS] <PACKAGES> ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-refresh\fR
Ask the registry and OSV again rather than reading the cache
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PACKAGES>\fR
The packages: ECOSYSTEM:NAME[@VERSION], or a NAME the project's lockfile or manifest places
.SH "JOUET DEPS AUDIT"
Check the project's locked dependencies against OSV

Reads the nearest Cargo.lock, npm\-family lockfile (package\-lock.json, pnpm\-lock.yaml, yarn.lock, bun.lock), uv.lock or poetry.lock, and go.mod, from here up to the repository's root, and asks OSV about every package version they lock from a public registry, in batches. Lists only the packages an advisory affects, each with its advisory IDs, severity and the first version that fixes it.

Exits 1 when any locked package is affected, 3 when there is no lockfile, and 5 when OSV cannot be reached.

Examples:
  jouet deps audit
  jouet deps audit \-\-ecosystem npm
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet deps audit [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-ecosystem\fR \fI<ECOSYSTEM>\fR
Only this ecosystem's lockfile: cargo, npm, pypi or go
.TP
\fB\-\-refresh\fR
Ask OSV again rather than reading the cache
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET DB"
Read a database's schema, or run a read\-only query, in SQLite or Postgres

The database is \-\-url, else DATABASE_URL, else DATABASE_URL from the project's fnox config (`fnox get`, read inside jouet and never printed). A .env file is never read. A SQLite database is a file path, `sqlite:` or `file:`; Postgres is a postgres:// URL or a `key=value` string. A password is shown as `***`.

Only local databases are opened: a Postgres host other than localhost, a loopback address or a Unix socket is refused (exit 4) without \-\-remote, so an agent does not read production by accident.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet db [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET DB SCHEMA"
List the tables with their columns, keys and indexes, or one table in full

Every table and view, one line each: columns with their types, `pk`, `not null`, defaults and `\-> table(column)` foreign keys, then the table's indexes. Cut to \-\-budget, with the names of the tables left out. Postgres's own schemas and partitions are left out.

With TABLE, that table alone, a column a line, with the foreign keys that point at it and its row count: exact when counting takes under half a second, else Postgres's estimate (`~`).

Exits 3 when TABLE does not exist.

Examples:
  jouet db schema
  jouet db schema users \-\-url app.db
  jouet db schema billing.invoices
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet db schema [OPTIONS] [<TABLE>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-url\fR \fI<URL>\fR
The database: a SQLite file, or a Postgres URL (default: DATABASE_URL)
.TP
\fB\-\-remote\fR
Allow a Postgres host that is not this machine
.TP
\fB\-\-budget\fR \fI<BYTES>\fR
The most bytes to print (default 8000; 0 is no limit)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<TABLE>\fR
One table or view to describe: NAME or SCHEMA.NAME
.SH "JOUET DB QUERY"
Run one read\-only query and print its rows, cut to a limit

Runs one statement that reads: SELECT, WITH, VALUES, TABLE, SHOW or EXPLAIN, and PRAGMA in SQLite. The database enforces it, not only the statement's first word: SQLite is opened read\-only and refuses a statement it says writes, and Postgres runs it alone in a READ ONLY transaction. Anything else, several statements, or a statement the database says writes, exits 4 with the reason, even with write rights.

Prints the first \-\-limit rows, then how many more there are (counted without reading them, up to 100,000). Each value is cut to \-\-width characters and the output to \-\-budget bytes, with a marker saying what was left out. NULL prints as NULL. With \-\-json, rows are arrays, with numbers, booleans and JSON as themselves.

Exits 1 when the database rejects the query (a syntax error, an unknown table), 5 when it cannot be reached, and 124 when the query runs past \-\-timeout.

Examples:
  jouet db query 'select * from users order by id desc'
  jouet db query 'select status, count(*) from jobs group by 1' \-\-url app.db
  jouet db query 'explain select * from orders where user_id = 7'
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet db query [OPTIONS] <SQL>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-limit\fR \fI<N>\fR
The most rows to print (default 50)
.TP
\fB\-\-width\fR \fI<CHARS>\fR
The most characters to print of one value (default 80; 0 is no limit)
.TP
\fB\-\-budget\fR \fI<BYTES>\fR
The most bytes to print (default 8000; 0 is no limit)
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
Stop the query after DUR, such as 30s or 2m (default 30s)
.TP
\fB\-\-url\fR \fI<URL>\fR
The database: a SQLite file, or a Postgres URL (default: DATABASE_URL)
.TP
\fB\-\-remote\fR
Allow a Postgres host that is not this machine
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<SQL>\fR
The statement to run, one only
.SH "JOUET REVIEW-PACKAGE"
Write one file for reviewing a range of commits

The file holds the commits, oldest first, a diffstat, and the full diff with \-\-context lines around each change. Refuses an empty range, and a BASE that is not an ancestor of HEAD (exit 4), since the diff would then show changes HEAD does not make. Written to `<git dir>/jouet/review\-BASE\-HEAD.md` unless \-o names a file (`\-` is stdout).
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet review\-package [OPTIONS] <BASE> [<HEAD>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-o, \-\-output\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
Write the package to FILE (`\-` is stdout)
.TP
\fB\-\-context\fR \fI<N>\fR
Lines of context around each change (default 10)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<BASE>\fR
The commit the review starts after, such as origin/main
.TP
\fB<HEAD>\fR
The last commit to review (default HEAD)
.SH "JOUET STACK"
Check a stack of PRs: does each layer really contain the one below

A stack is a chain of PRs, each based on the branch of the one below. The chain comes from gh\-stack's metadata when it tracks the branch, else from the PRs' bases on GitHub. Branches are read as they are locally and as of the last fetch; nothing is fetched or changed.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet stack [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET STACK STATUS"
Show each layer's PR and whether it contains its parent, by ancestry and content

For each layer: its PR, base and head, unpushed local commits, and whether it contains the layer below. That is checked by ancestry (the parent's tip is in its history) and by content: every file the parent layer changed must have that change in the layer's tree, so a restack that kept the commits but reverted their changes is caught. A layer whose prerequisite merged needs the trunk merged in. A layer's local branch is used when there is one, since it may hold unpushed fixes. Exits 1 when a layer needs attention, 3 when the branch is in no stack (no PR, and gh\-stack does not track it), 5 when GitHub cannot be reached.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet stack status [OPTIONS] [<BRANCH>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<BRANCH>\fR
A branch in the stack (default: the current branch)
.SH "JOUET STACK SYNC"
Bring each layer up to date by merging its parent, verified, never rebasing

Fetches, then goes up the stack from the bottom. Each layer starts from its local branch, never the remote's, so unpushed fixes are kept; a local branch behind its remote is refused. A layer that lacks its parent gets the parent merged in, or the trunk once its prerequisite has merged (a squash merge included). Each merge is checked before it is committed: every change it brings in must be in the merged tree. Sync stops at the first layer that conflicts, fails that check, is checked out in another worktree, or has uncommitted changes, and says what to do. A branch checked out nowhere is merged in a temporary worktree; the one checked out here is merged in place. Never rebases or force\-pushes; pushes only with \-\-push, plainly. Exits 1 on a conflict or a failed check, 4 when refused before merging, 5 when a fetch or push fails.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet stack sync [OPTIONS] [<BRANCH>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be merged; merge, fetch and push nothing
.TP
\fB\-\-push\fR
Push the layers ahead of their remote branch afterwards (never forced)
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-fetch\fR
Use the remote branches as last fetched
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<BRANCH>\fR
A branch in the stack (default: the current branch)
.SH "JOUET SEARCH"
Search files like rg, within a budget, grouped by symbol

Embeds ripgrep's walker and searcher, so it respects .gitignore, skips hidden and binary files, and is about as fast as rg. Patterns are rg's regular expressions (\-F for a literal). Beyond rg:

\- Matches are grouped by file, then by the function, method, class or
  module they are in (for Rust, Python, JavaScript, TypeScript, Go,
  Ruby, Java, C and C++).
\- Output stops at a byte budget, in total and per file, and says what it
  left out: `... 4 more in src/x.rs`, and a closing line naming the files
  it never reached. Nothing is cut silently; \-\-budget 0 removes a limit.
\- Inside an agent session (JOUET_SESSION_ID, CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID or
  CODEX_THREAD_ID), a match this session was already shown, in a file
  that has not changed since, collapses to one line (`src/x.rs: 3 already
  shown, unchanged`). \-\-again shows them anyway.

With \-\-structural, PATTERN is code in \-\-lang, matched by ast\-grep: `$X` is one node, `$$$X` any number, and only files in that language are searched. Quote it in single quotes, or the shell expands `$X` (`jouet agent\-help edit` has more on patterns).

Exits 0 with matches, 1 with none, 2 for a bad pattern or glob, and 3 for a path that does not exist.

Examples:
  jouet search 'fn download' src/
  jouet search \-F 'client.get(' \-g '*.rs'
  jouet search \-i timeout \-\-budget 2000 \-\-file\-budget 400
  jouet search \-l TODO
  jouet search \-\-structural \-\-lang rust '$X.unwrap()'
  jouet search \-\-structural \-\-lang go 'if err != nil { return $$$ }'
  jouet search \-\-structural \-\-lang typescript 'useEffect($FN, [])'
  jouet search \-\-structural \-\-lang rust '$F($$$A)' \-\-callers\-of src/config.rs#load
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet search [OPTIONS] <PATTERN> [<PATHS>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-F, \-\-fixed\-strings\fR
Treat the pattern as a literal string
.TP
\fB\-i, \-\-ignore\-case\fR
Match case\-insensitively
.TP
\fB\-w, \-\-word\-regexp\fR
Match whole words only
.TP
\fB\-g, \-\-glob\fR \fI<GLOB>\fR
Include or exclude files, as rg's \-g: `*.rs`, `!*_test.go`. Repeatable
.TP
\fB\-\-hidden\fR
Search hidden files and directories too
.TP
\fB\-l, \-\-files\-with\-matches\fR
List the matching files with their match counts, not the lines
.TP
\fB\-\-budget\fR \fI<BYTES>\fR
The most bytes to print in total (default 6000; 0 is no limit)
.TP
\fB\-\-file\-budget\fR \fI<BYTES>\fR
The most bytes to print from one file (default 1500; 0 is no limit)
.TP
\fB\-\-again\fR
Show matches this session was already shown, too
.TP
\fB\-\-structural\fR
Match PATTERN as code, with ast\-grep: `$X` is one node, `$$$X` any number. Needs \-\-lang
.TP
\fB\-\-lang\fR \fI<LANG>\fR
The language of a \-\-structural pattern: rust, python, javascript, typescript, tsx, go, ruby, java, c or cpp
.TP
\fB\-\-callers\-of\fR \fI<PATH#SYMBOL>\fR
With \-\-structural, only the calls the code graph attributes to this symbol, given as PATH#NAME (or #ID from `jouet graph symbols`)
.TP
\fB\-\-impact\fR \fI<PATH#SYMBOL>\fR
With \-\-structural, only matches inside this symbol and the code the graph says depends on it, three hops back
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PATTERN>\fR
The regular expression to search for (a literal with \-F)
.TP
\fB<PATHS>\fR
Files and directories to search (default .)
.SH "JOUET TEST"
Run the test suite, or only the tests a change affects

Without \-\-affected, runs the repository's test task as the full suite: a mise `test` task, a package.json `test` script, Cargo.toml (`cargo test \-\-workspace`), a justfile or Makefile `test` target or go.mod, else the whole suite of each test runner found. With \-\-affected, runs only the tests that the changes since the merge base with \-\-since (default: the upstream, else the default branch), uncommitted and untracked files included, affect. Adapters delegate to each runner's own selection: vitest's and jest's module graphs, pytest\-testmon when it is installed, else a static import graph. The whole suite runs instead when a lockfile, tool versions, CI, the test configuration, a conftest or setup file, or a file no adapter can trace changed; nothing runs for docs only. Each test is listed with the chain of imports that picked it, and the command. Affected runs count as partial: the full suite still has to pass before you finish or push. Words after `\-\-` go to the runner.

With \-\-cmd, the words after `\-\-` are the whole command instead, run directly rather than through a shell: one test, an e2e suite or a build, such as `jouet test \-\-cmd \-\- cargo build`.

Every run's output, stdout and stderr together, goes to a log under jouet's log directory, kept for a week. For an agent (and in plain display), that is all it goes to: a passing run prints one line with the counts and the log's path, and a failing one each failure's name and the lines that explain it (the assertion, the panic, the compiler error), up to \-\-budget bytes, then the names of the failures that did not fit. Read more from the log rather than running it again. For a person at a terminal, the output streams as it runs. Failures are recognised in the output of cargo test and nextest, rustc, tsc, go test and go build, pytest, unittest, jest, vitest and TAP (bats); for any other output, a failing run shows the end of it.

Exits 1 when tests fail, 2 for a usage error (the runner's too), 4 when no runner is found or installed.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet test [OPTIONS] [<EXTRA>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-affected\fR
Run only the tests the changes affect
.TP
\fB\-\-since\fR \fI<REF>\fR
Measure changes from the merge base with REF (default: the upstream, else the default branch)
.TP
\fB\-\-adapter\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
Use only this adapter: vitest, jest, pytest or graph (Go, Ruby and Java, from the code graph)
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Print what would run and why, running nothing
.TP
\fB\-\-cmd\fR
Run the words after `\-\-` as the command, instead of the test task
.TP
\fB\-\-budget\fR \fI<BYTES>\fR
Bytes of failure text to show (default 4000; 0 shows every failure whole)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<EXTRA>\fR
Arguments for the test runner, after `\-\-`; with \-\-cmd, the command
.SH "JOUET WAIT"
Block until a condition holds, then print one summary

Replaces `sleep N; check; repeat` loops. The first check is immediate; later ones back off from \-\-interval to 60s and speed up again when the state changes. Exits 0 when the condition holds and 124 on timeout, where running the same command again keeps waiting. In human mode a live status line is shown on stderr; otherwise each change of state is one line on stderr, so a backgrounded wait's log stays small.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wait [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
Give up after DUR, such as 90s, 10m or 1h30m (default 30m)
.TP
\fB\-\-interval\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
Time between the first checks; backs off to 60s (default 10s)
.TP
\fB\-\-confirm\-conflict\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
How long a conflict GitHub reports must persist before `wait ci` and `wait pr` believe it (default 10s). For tests
.TP
\fB\-\-jsonl\fR
Print one JSON event per line, then the result document
.TP
\fB\-\-quiet\fR
Print no progress and no text result, only the exit code (and JSON when asked for)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET WAIT CI"
Wait for a PR's checks, and show why any failed

Follows the PR head, switching to a new head when one is pushed, unless \-\-sha pins a commit. Returns on the first failed check unless \-\-all. Skipped and neutral checks count as passing. A conflicted PR returns `conflict` (exit 4), since pull_request workflows will not run, once GitHub reports it again 10s later (right after a push it can still answer for the old head); no checks within \-\-appear\-timeout returns `no_checks` (exit 1). For each failed Actions job or GitLab job, the failed step's log is excerpted and the full log saved. On GitLab the checks are the head's pipeline jobs and commit statuses, on Forgejo its commit statuses. Waits on the same PR share their state, so they poll the forge once.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wait ci [OPTIONS] [<PR>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-repo\fR \fI<OWNER/NAME>\fR
The repository for a bare number or branch. Defaults to the git remote
.TP
\fB\-\-sha\fR \fI<SHA>\fR
Wait on this commit's checks instead of following the PR head
.TP
\fB\-\-required\fR
Only checks that branch protection requires
.TP
\fB\-\-check\fR \fI<GLOB>\fR
Only checks whose name matches GLOB (`*` and `?`). Repeatable
.TP
\fB\-\-ignore\fR \fI<GLOB>\fR
Leave out checks whose name matches GLOB. Repeatable
.TP
\fB\-\-all\fR
Wait for every check to finish, even after one fails
.TP
\fB\-\-log\-lines\fR \fI<N>\fR
Lines of each failure log to show (default 60; 0 shows the whole failed step)
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-logs\fR
Do not fetch failure logs
.TP
\fB\-\-appear\-timeout\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
How long checks may take to appear on a commit (default 3m)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PR>\fR
The PR: a number, URL, OWNER/REPO#N or branch. Defaults to the current branch's PR
.SH "JOUET WAIT RUN"
Wait for a GitHub Actions run or a GitLab pipeline

Returns when the run completes, or on its first failed job, with the failed step's log excerpted as for `wait ci`. Forgejo has no runs jouet can read, so it exits 4 there.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wait run [OPTIONS] <RUN>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-repo\fR \fI<OWNER/NAME>\fR
The repository for a bare run ID. Defaults to the git remote
.TP
\fB\-\-log\-lines\fR \fI<N>\fR
Lines of each failure log to show (default 60; 0 shows the whole failed step)
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-logs\fR
Do not fetch failure logs
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<RUN>\fR
The run ID, or its Actions or GitLab pipeline URL
.SH "JOUET WAIT PR"
Wait for a PR to become mergeable, merged, approved, ready, ...

\-\-until is one of: mergeable (keeps waiting while GitHub reports UNKNOWN; exit 1 on a conflict), merged (exit 1 if closed unmerged), closed, approved (exit 1 when changes are requested), checks\-registered (any check on the head), ready (no `pr status` attention codes and no pending checks; exit 1 on conflict, checks_failing, changes_requested or unresolved_threads), or any\-change (the next push, check result, review, thread or state change, printed as one event). A conflict counts once GitHub reports it again 10s later, since right after a push it can still answer for the old head.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wait pr [OPTIONS] [<PR>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-repo\fR \fI<OWNER/NAME>\fR
The repository for a bare number or branch. Defaults to the git remote
.TP
\fB\-\-until\fR \fI<CONDITION>\fR
The condition to wait for
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PR>\fR
The PR: a number, URL, OWNER/REPO#N or branch. Defaults to the current branch's PR
.SH "JOUET WAIT FILE"
Wait for a file to exist, contain a match, or be gone
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wait file [OPTIONS] <PATH>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-contains\fR \fI<REGEX>\fR
Wait until a line of the file matches REGEX, and print that line
.TP
\fB\-\-gone\fR
Wait until nothing exists at PATH
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PATH>\fR
The file (or directory) to watch
.SH "JOUET WAIT LOG"
Wait for a line matching a regex to be appended to a log

Follows the path like `tail \-F`: a file that is rotated or truncated is read again from its start, and one that does not exist yet is waited for. Starts at the current end of the file and prints the matching line.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wait log [OPTIONS] <PATH>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-match\fR \fI<REGEX>\fR
The regex a line must match
.TP
\fB\-\-from\-start\fR
Also search what the file already holds
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PATH>\fR
The log file to follow
.SH "JOUET WAIT PORT"
Wait for a TCP port to accept connections, or to stop
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wait port [OPTIONS] <ADDR>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-closed\fR
Wait until the port refuses connections instead
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ADDR>\fR
HOST:PORT, or just PORT for localhost
.SH "JOUET WAIT HTTP"
Wait for a URL to answer with a status, and optionally a match

Any 2xx status counts unless \-\-status says otherwise. Redirects are followed, except when \-\-status asks for a 3xx. \-\-expect takes the assertions `jouet http` does, such as `jsonpath $.ready == true`, and waits until all of them hold.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wait http [OPTIONS] <URL>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-status\fR \fI<CODE>\fR
The status code to wait for (default: any 2xx)
.TP
\fB\-\-contains\fR \fI<REGEX>\fR
Also wait until a line of the body matches REGEX, and print that line
.TP
\fB\-\-expect\fR \fI<ASSERTION>\fR
Also wait until ASSERTION holds, as in `jouet http \-\-expect` (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<URL>\fR
The http:// or https:// URL to GET
.SH "JOUET WAIT PID"
Wait for a process to exit
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wait pid [OPTIONS] <PID>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<PID>\fR
The process ID
.SH "JOUET WAIT CMD"
Re\-run a command until it exits with the expected code

Runs CMD directly, not through a shell, every \-\-interval. A run still going at the timeout is stopped, though every run gets at least one \-\-interval, so `\-\-timeout 0` runs it once. Prints the last run's exit code and the tail of its output, and saves the full output to a log.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wait cmd [OPTIONS] [<CMD>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-expect\-exit\fR \fI<N>\fR
The exit code to wait for (default 0)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<CMD>\fR
The command and its arguments, after `\-\-`
.SH "JOUET WAIT JOB"
Wait for a `jouet job` to end, or to be ready

\-\-until exited (the default) is met when the job exits 0 or `jouet job stop` stopped it, and fails (exit 1) when it exits with another code. \-\-until ready is met while it runs and its ready check passes: \-\-port or \-\-http, else the one it was started with. A job that ends before it is ready fails. On a failure or a timeout, the end of its log is shown. Exits 3 when this work tree has no such job.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wait job [OPTIONS] <ID>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-until\fR \fI<CONDITION>\fR
`exited` (the default) or `ready`
.TP
\fB\-\-port\fR \fI<N>\fR
Ready once localhost's port N accepts connections
.TP
\fB\-\-http\fR \fI<URL>\fR
Ready once URL answers with a 2xx status
.TP
\fB\-\-log\-lines\fR \fI<N>\fR
Lines of its log to show on failure or timeout (default 60; 0 shows all of it)
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-logs\fR
Do not show its log
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ID>\fR
The job's ID
.SH "JOUET WAIT DAEMON"
Wait for a pitchfork daemon to be ready, or to stop

For a project's long\-running services, the daemons pitchfork runs (one\-off work is `wait job`'s). Reads `pitchfork status ID \-\-json` in this directory, so a short ID resolves in this project's namespace. Ready is `running` and, when there is a port to try (\-\-port, or the port pitchfork says the daemon binds) or a URL (\-\-http), answering there: pitchfork's JSON does not say whether its own ready check passed. \-\-until stopped waits until the daemon no longer runs. A daemon that errors or fails ends the wait with exit 1; then, and on a timeout, the end of its log is shown and the whole log is saved. Exits 3 when pitchfork has no such daemon and 4 when pitchfork is not installed.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wait daemon [OPTIONS] <ID>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-until\fR \fI<CONDITION>\fR
`ready` (the default) or `stopped`
.TP
\fB\-\-port\fR \fI<N>\fR
Ready once localhost's port N accepts connections
.TP
\fB\-\-http\fR \fI<URL>\fR
Ready once URL answers with a 2xx status
.TP
\fB\-\-log\-lines\fR \fI<N>\fR
Lines of its log to show on failure or timeout (default 60; 0 shows all of it)
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-logs\fR
Do not read its log
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ID>\fR
The daemon's ID: `api`, or `namespace/api`
.SH "JOUET HTTP"
Send an HTTP request and print the status and only the JSON paths asked for, within a budget

Prints the status, the content type, size and time, the headers worth acting on (location, retry\-after, rate limits), and the body cut to \-\-budget bytes with a marker naming what was left out and the file the whole body is saved in. With \-\-select, it prints only the values at those JSONPaths (RFC 9535) instead of the body, so nothing needs piping through jq or Python. JSON is printed compact for agents and indented for people.

\-\-expect checks the response in hurl's style: a query (`status`, `header NAME`, `jsonpath PATH`, `body`, `duration` in milliseconds), an optional `count` and `not`, then a predicate (`==`, `!=`, `>`, `>=`, `<`, `<=`, `contains`, `startsWith`, `endsWith`, `matches`, `exists`, `isEmpty`) and its value. Without a `status` assertion, a status of 400 or more fails.

Secrets are never printed: Authorization, cookies, and headers and JSON keys named like a token, password, API key or session are redacted, and so is any value of `${NAME}`, which jouet expands from its own environment in the URL, headers and an inline body. Give credentials that way, from the environment or through `fnox exec \-\- jouet http ...`, not written into the command; in a project that uses fnox, its secrets' values are redacted too.

Exits 1 when an assertion fails, 3 when a \-\-data or \-\-json\-body file is missing, 4 when a `${NAME}` is not set, 5 when the server cannot be reached, and 124 when it does not answer within \-\-timeout.

Examples:
  jouet http GET https://api.github.com/repos/jdx/mise \-\-select '$.stargazers_count'
  jouet http GET localhost:3000/health \-\-expect 'jsonpath $.ok == true'
  jouet http POST https://api.example.com/items \-\-json\-body '{"name":"x"}' \-\-expect status=201
  fnox exec \-\- jouet http GET https://api.example.com/me \-H 'Authorization: Bearer ${API_TOKEN}'
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet http [OPTIONS] <METHOD> <URL>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-H, \-\-header\fR \fI<NAME:VALUE>\fR
A request header, NAME:VALUE (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-\-json\-body\fR \fI<JSON>\fR
Send JSON (checked to be JSON) with Content\-Type: application/json; @FILE reads it from a file
.TP
\fB\-d, \-\-data\fR \fI<DATA>\fR
Send this text as the body; @FILE reads a file and @\- stdin
.TP
\fB\-s, \-\-select\fR \fI<JSONPATH>\fR
Print only the values at this JSONPath, such as `$.items[*].name` (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-e, \-\-expect\fR \fI<ASSERTION>\fR
Check the response, as in `status == 200` or `jsonpath $.ok == true`, and exit 1 if it fails (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-\-budget\fR \fI<BYTES>\fR
The most bytes of body or selected values to print (default 4000; 0 is no limit)
.TP
\fB\-i, \-\-headers\fR
Print every response header, not only those worth acting on
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-follow\fR
Do not follow redirects, so a 3xx is the answer
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
Give up after DUR, such as 10s or 2m (default 30s)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<METHOD>\fR
GET, HEAD, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE or OPTIONS
.TP
\fB<URL>\fR
The URL; one without a scheme is taken as http:// for localhost and https:// otherwise
.SH "JOUET JOB"
Run one\-off background work, such as a build or a server for one check

A job is a command running in the background, detached in a process group of its own, with its output in one log. Jobs belong to the work tree they were started in. No daemon runs: each job is a file under jouet's state directory. Unix only; on Windows, start and stop exit 4.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet job [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET JOB START"
Start a command in the background and return once it is ready

Runs CMD (directly, not through a shell) in this directory, with stdin closed and stdout and stderr in the job's log. With \-\-port, \-\-http or \-\-output, waits up to \-\-timeout (default 60s) for the job to be ready and fails (exit 1) with the end of its log if it exits first; a job still not ready at the timeout keeps running (exit 124). Without one, it returns at once, unless the command fails straight away. Refuses (exit 4) while a job of the same ID runs, unless \-\-replace stops it first.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet job start [OPTIONS] <ID> [<CMD>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-port\fR \fI<N>\fR
Ready once localhost's port N accepts connections
.TP
\fB\-\-http\fR \fI<URL>\fR
Ready once URL answers with a 2xx status
.TP
\fB\-o, \-\-output\fR \fI<REGEX>\fR
Ready once a line of the job's log matches REGEX
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
How long to wait for the ready check (default 60s)
.TP
\fB\-\-replace\fR
Stop a running job of the same ID first
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ID>\fR
The job's ID: letters, digits, `_`, `.` and `\-`
.TP
\fB<CMD>\fR
The command and its arguments, after `\-\-`
.SH "JOUET JOB LS"
List this work tree's jobs: running, exited, stopped or gone

`gone` is a job that is no longer running with nothing recorded about how it ended. Jobs that ended more than a week ago are removed.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet job ls [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET JOB LOGS"
Print the end of a job's log
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet job logs [OPTIONS] <ID>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-n, \-\-lines\fR \fI<N>\fR
Lines to print (default 50; 0 prints all of it)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ID>\fR
The job's ID
.SH "JOUET JOB STOP"
Stop jobs: SIGTERM to the whole process group, then SIGKILL

A job that has already ended is left as it is.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet job stop [OPTIONS] [<IDS>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-grace\fR \fI<DUR>\fR
How long to wait after SIGTERM before SIGKILL (default 5s)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<IDS>\fR
The jobs' IDs
.SH "JOUET CI"
Run GitHub Actions jobs here, or say whether a failure on GitHub is flaky
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet ci [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET CI RUN"
Run a workflow's jobs here with jactionlint, and show why any failed

Runs `jactionlint run`, which needs no container engine: each job runs in a copy of the checkout, in the OS sandbox, with a throwaway home and no secrets unless given. Prints one line for the run, then each failed step with the end of its output (cut like `wait ci`'s log excerpts) and the jobs that were skipped and why; the whole transcript is kept in a log. WORKFLOW is a file of .github/workflows (the extension is optional), a path, or a workflow's `name:`. \-\-check takes a check's name as `wait ci` prints it and runs the job behind it. Arguments after `\-\-` go to `jactionlint run` as they are, such as `\-\-secret NAME=VALUE`. `\-\-secrets\-from fnox` resolves the `secrets.*` the jobs read through fnox and passes them in a temporary file, never on the command line. It shows what happens on this machine, not whether GitHub will pass: jobs for another OS are skipped. Exits 1 when a job failed, 3 when no workflow or job matches, 4 when jactionlint is missing or the run could not start.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet ci run [OPTIONS] [<WORKFLOW>] [<ARGS>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-j, \-\-job\fR \fI<ID>\fR
Run only this job and the jobs it needs. Repeatable
.TP
\fB\-\-check\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
Run the job behind this check name, such as `unit (ubuntu\-latest)`
.TP
\fB\-e, \-\-event\fR \fI<EVENT>\fR
The event that triggers the workflow (default push)
.TP
\fB\-\-log\-lines\fR \fI<N>\fR
Lines of each failed step to show (default 60; 0 shows all of it)
.TP
\fB\-\-secrets\-from\fR \fI<SOURCE>\fR
Give the jobs the secrets they read, resolved through this tool
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<WORKFLOW>\fR
The workflow. Needed when the repository has several and no \-\-check is given
.TP
\fB<ARGS>\fR
Arguments for `jactionlint run`, after `\-\-`
.SH "JOUET CI EXPLAIN"
Say whether each failed job is flaky, infrastructure or real, and rerun only a flake

For each failed GitHub Actions job of a PR's latest runs, a run or one job: the verdict, the evidence for it, the failed step and an excerpt of its log. The log is read with fixed patterns for failed tests (cargo, nextest, bats, pytest, vitest, jest, go test), compile and lint errors, and runner or network trouble; history comes from the workflow's recent runs. flaky: it passed on the same commit elsewhere (another attempt, or another OS's job, which the evidence names), or the same test failed on another branch that passed later. infrastructure: the runner, disk, network, a registry, a rate limit, a service container or the cache. real: a compile or lint error, a test that failed on every attempt on this commit, or one that fails the same way on the default branch, which the evidence says is not the change's fault. Anything else is unknown. A job that failed or was cancelled only because another did is listed as following it. \-\-rerun reruns the failed jobs when every one is flaky or infrastructure, and refuses (exit 4) otherwise. A second failure on the same commit turns a flake real. Exits 0 when nothing failed or every failure is flaky or infrastructure, 1 when one is real or unknown, 3 when the PR, run or job does not exist, 4 when nothing has failed yet in a run still going. What it fetches is cached under the state dir, so explaining the same run again is cheap.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet ci explain [OPTIONS] [<TARGET>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-repo\fR \fI<OWNER/NAME>\fR
The repository for a bare number, branch, \-\-run or \-\-job. Defaults to the git remote
.TP
\fB\-\-run\fR \fI<ID>\fR
Explain this Actions run instead of a PR
.TP
\fB\-\-job\fR \fI<ID>\fR
Explain this one Actions job
.TP
\fB\-\-rerun\fR
Rerun the failed jobs if every one is flaky or infrastructure
.TP
\fB\-\-log\-lines\fR \fI<N>\fR
Lines of each failure log to show (default 40; 0 shows the whole failed step)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<TARGET>\fR
The PR: a number, URL, OWNER/REPO#N or branch, or an Actions run or job URL. Defaults to the current branch's PR
.SH "JOUET RULE"
List, test and trust the rules jouet's hooks run

Rules are `[[rule]]` tables in TOML files: built in, in ~/.config/jouet/rules, and in a repository's .jouet/rules once it is trusted. `jouet rule schema` prints their JSON schema; a rule with logic a matcher cannot express names a Luau `script`, written against the API `jouet rule types` prints.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet rule [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET RULE LIST"
List every rule, where it comes from and whether it runs
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet rule list [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET RULE TEST"
Run a rule against a command or hook payload, or against its examples

With neither \-\-command nor \-\-payload, runs the rule's `examples`: every `fires` example must make it deny, warn or record, and every `quiet` one must leave it silent. It fails, showing what was expected and what the rule did, when one does not, or when the rule lacks either kind. \-\-all does this for every rule. \-\-replay runs the rule over every call in the hook ledger instead, and fails if it fires on none of them.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet rule test [OPTIONS] [<RULE>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-all\fR
Run the examples of every rule: built in, yours and this repository's
.TP
\fB\-\-command\fR \fI<CMD>\fR
A shell command to test the rule against
.TP
\fB\-\-payload\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
A Claude Code or Codex hook payload (JSON) to test against; `\-` is stdin
.TP
\fB\-\-exit\-code\fR \fI<N>\fR
The command's exit code, for a post\-tool\-use rule (default 0)
.TP
\fB\-\-replay\fR
Run the rule over the hook ledger's calls: what it fires on, and the near misses
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<RULE>\fR
A rule's name, or the path to a .toml (or .luau) rule file
.SH "JOUET RULE SCHEMA"
Print the JSON schema of rule files

Every key a `[[rule]]` table takes, with what it means. The rule files jouet writes start with a `#:schema` line, so editors that read it complete and check them.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet rule schema [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET RULE TYPES"
Print the Luau type definitions of the jouet module, for rule scripts
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet rule types [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET RULE TRUST"
Trust this repository's .jouet/rules as they are now
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet rule trust [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET RULE UNTRUST"
Stop trusting this repository's .jouet/rules
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet rule untrust [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET RULE NEW"
Write a new rule of yours, in warn mode, from a template or a winding note

Writes ~/.config/jouet/rules/NAME.toml, a rule saying why it exists, who wrote it and when, with a matcher and examples to fill in. It starts in warn mode: `jouet rule promote` makes its denials real. \-\-script adds NAME.luau for logic a matcher cannot express. With \-\-from\-note, the rule is the one `jouet wind` drafted.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet rule new [OPTIONS] <NAME>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-from\-note\fR \fI<ID>\fR
Start from the rule a winding note drafted, such as n3
.TP
\fB\-\-why\fR \fI<TEXT>\fR
What the rule is for, in one line (the template's `why`)
.TP
\fB\-\-author\fR \fI<WHO>\fR
Who is writing it: your session's id (default: $JOUET_SESSION_ID, or the harness's)
.TP
\fB\-\-script\fR
Also write NAME.luau, a script for logic a matcher cannot express
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<NAME>\fR
The rule's name: lowercase words joined by dashes
.SH "JOUET RULE PROMOTE"
Switch a rule of yours from warn to deny

Only rules in ~/.config/jouet/rules: built\-in rules are part of jouet, and a repository's change through a reviewed PR. Logged in `jouet rule log`.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet rule promote [OPTIONS] <NAME>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-reason\fR \fI<TEXT>\fR
Why, for the log
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<NAME>\fR
The rule's name
.SH "JOUET RULE LOG"
Show every change made to your rules, and why
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet rule log [OPTIONS] [<NAME>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<NAME>\fR
Only this rule's changes
.SH "JOUET RULE GAP"
Record something a rule could not express, for `jouet share \-\-idea`

jouet records on its own when a rule reads a `jouet` primitive that does not exist. This records a wish in your words: "I kept doing X; there is no primitive for Y".
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet rule gap [OPTIONS] <TEXT>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-rule\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
The rule you were writing, if any
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<TEXT>\fR
What you wanted a rule to do, and what was missing
.SH "JOUET RUN"
Run a routine: a Luau program on jouet's primitives (also `joue`)

Routines are Luau programs, where rules are TOML configuration. `joue NAME [ARGS]` is the same as `jouet run NAME [ARGS]`. Routines are built in (land, pr\-next, ship\-check, sweep), in ~/.config/jouet/routines, and in a repository's .jouet/routines once it is trusted with \-\-trust. Each declares its arguments, so `joue NAME \-\-help` says what it takes, and the capabilities it reaches; anything else is refused (exit 4). A routine that writes acts only with \-\-yes: without it every write is previewed, and joue exits 4 (0 with \-\-dry\-run).
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet run [OPTIONS] [COMMAND]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-list\fR
List every routine, where it comes from and what it reaches
.TP
\fB\-\-trust\fR
Trust this repository's .jouet/routines as they are now
.TP
\fB\-\-untrust\fR
Stop trusting this repository's .jouet/routines
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET RUN TEST"
Run routines' examples against their recorded responses

Each example runs the routine with its arguments against a fixture: a local fake GitHub serves its recorded responses, and jouet's commands, git, mise and the ledger answer from it too. Nothing real is reached, and file edits are only previewed. Fails (exit 1) when an example's exit code, output, JSON, previewed writes or requests differ from what it expects, or when a routine has no examples.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet run test [OPTIONS] [<ROUTINE>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ROUTINE>\fR
The routine (default: every one)
.SH "JOUET RUN TYPES"
Print the Luau type definitions of the primitives
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet run types [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET WIND"
Notice costly patterns in the hook ledger and whole sessions, and propose fixes

Reads the ledger jouet's hooks keep from where it last stopped. When a command keeps failing across sessions, or agents keep sleeping instead of waiting, it drafts a rule in warn mode and keeps a note of it, which the next session start shows (two at most). The hooks run it on their own; running it by hand reads the ledger now. Off with JOUET_WIND=0.

The ledger holds only command shapes. `jouet wind digest` and `jouet wind review` read whole sessions: the user's corrections, abandoned approaches, waits and repeated exploration. A review's proposals (mise tasks, rules, routines, AGENTS.md or memory notes) are recorded with `jouet wind propose` and shown like the ledger's notes.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wind [OPTIONS] [COMMAND]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET WIND SHOW"
Show a note or a proposal: what was noticed, and what was drafted
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wind show [OPTIONS] <ID>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ID>\fR
The id of a note (n3), a proposal (p2), a suggested review (r1) or a mise task (t1)
.SH "JOUET WIND DISMISS"
Drop a note, a proposal or a suggested review, so it is never shown again
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wind dismiss [OPTIONS] <ID>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ID>\fR
The id of a note (n3), a proposal (p2), a suggested review (r1) or a mise task (t1)
.SH "JOUET WIND ADOPT"
Mark a review's proposal as applied
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wind adopt [OPTIONS] <ID>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ID>\fR
The id of a note (n3), a proposal (p2), a suggested review (r1) or a mise task (t1)
.SH "JOUET WIND DIGEST"
Digest whole sessions: corrections, failures, waits, re\-reads

Reads a Claude Code or Codex transcript and reduces it to a few thousand tokens: the user's corrections and interruptions, failures and their retries, long calls, where tokens and time went (as `jouet audit` counts them), files read repeatedly, commands typed out again, and a timeline with a phase per prompt. Every item names its lines in the transcript. Secrets are redacted (with gitleaks' rules too, when a config is found) and the home directory is written as ~. Nothing leaves the machine. `jouet wind review` prints the same with a prompt for a reviewing agent.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wind digest [OPTIONS] [<SESSION>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-last\fR
The most recently written session (inside a session, that session)
.TP
\fB\-\-since\fR \fI<TIME>\fR
Every session written since TIME (7d, 12h, a date or RFC 3339), the 8 most recent
.TP
\fB\-\-harness\fR \fI<HARNESS>\fR
Which transcripts to read: claude, codex or all (default all)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Only sessions that ran in PATH or a directory below it
.TP
\fB\-\-budget\fR \fI<N>\fR
Tokens per session's digest, about (default 4000)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<SESSION>\fR
A session ID, a prefix of one, or a transcript's path
.SH "JOUET WIND REVIEW"
Digest whole sessions: corrections, failures, waits, re\-reads

Reads a Claude Code or Codex transcript and reduces it to a few thousand tokens: the user's corrections and interruptions, failures and their retries, long calls, where tokens and time went (as `jouet audit` counts them), files read repeatedly, commands typed out again, and a timeline with a phase per prompt. Every item names its lines in the transcript. Secrets are redacted (with gitleaks' rules too, when a config is found) and the home directory is written as ~. Nothing leaves the machine. `jouet wind review` prints the same with a prompt for a reviewing agent.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wind review [OPTIONS] [<SESSION>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-last\fR
The most recently written session (inside a session, that session)
.TP
\fB\-\-since\fR \fI<TIME>\fR
Every session written since TIME (7d, 12h, a date or RFC 3339), the 8 most recent
.TP
\fB\-\-harness\fR \fI<HARNESS>\fR
Which transcripts to read: claude, codex or all (default all)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Only sessions that ran in PATH or a directory below it
.TP
\fB\-\-budget\fR \fI<N>\fR
Tokens per session's digest, about (default 4000)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<SESSION>\fR
A session ID, a prefix of one, or a transcript's path
.SH "JOUET WIND PROPOSE"
Record what a review proposed, to show at the next session start

Reads a review's JSON reply on stdin (or from FILE): `{"session": "...", "proposals": [{"kind", "title", "why", "evidence", "scope", "draft"}]}`, as `jouet wind review` asks for. Each proposal is checked and recorded as pending: three per review and three a day at most, never one that looks like it holds a secret, never one already proposed. Recording applies nothing: the next session starts show each proposal in one line, and the agent there asks the user before applying it.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet wind propose [OPTIONS] [<FILE>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<FILE>\fR
Read the reply from FILE instead of stdin
.SH "JOUET UNWIND"
Propose retiring rules that are noisy or always overridden, and unused tasks

Lists your rules and a trusted repository's that the hook ledger shows are nearly always overridden, fire on a large share of calls, or no longer load. Built\-in rules are never proposed. \-\-apply moves your own rules to ~/.config/jouet/rules/retired/ and logs it; a repository's rules change through a PR. It also lists the mise tasks `jouet task apply` added that no agent session has run in the 21 days since, and still run what jouet wrote; \-\-apply removes them with `mise tasks rm`.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet unwind [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-apply\fR
Retire the proposed rules of yours, and remove the proposed tasks
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET TASK"
Propose mise tasks for commands agents keep repeating, and add one

`jouet task propose` reads this repository's recent session transcripts (Claude Code and Codex) and drafts a mise task for each command sequence, or long command, that agents ran at least 3 times in 2 sessions and no task already runs: its name, `run`, description, `depends` and `sources` where they can be inferred, how often it ran, and which file it should go in (mise.local.toml or mise.toml) with why. The hook ledger notices such sequences too, and a session start then names them. `jouet task apply ID \-\-file local|shared` adds the task through `mise tasks add`, never by editing TOML; which file is the user's choice, so ask them first. Applying needs mise 2026.10.25 or later.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet task [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET TASK PROPOSE"
Draft mise tasks for commands agents repeat in this repository

Prints every pending proposal for the repository: the task, the evidence (runs, sessions, time, output tokens), the file jouet suggests and why, and the question to ask the user. Proposals are kept with winding's notes: `jouet wind show ID` prints one and `jouet wind dismiss ID` drops it.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet task propose [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-since\fR \fI<TIME>\fR
Read the sessions written since TIME (7d, 12h, a date or RFC 3339), the 8 most recent (default 14d)
.TP
\fB\-\-harness\fR \fI<HARNESS>\fR
Which transcripts to read: claude, codex or all (default all)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET TASK APPLY"
Add a proposed task through mise, to the file the user chose

Runs `mise tasks add \-\-json` (or `mise tasks set` for a task jouet added before) with `\-\-env local` for mise.local.toml or `\-\-path` to the repository's shared config for mise.toml, and prints the JSON mise returns. Without \-\-file it refuses (exit 4) and says which file jouet suggests: ask the user, then pass their choice. A mise older than 2026.10.25 is refused (exit 4). A command with shell syntax (`&&`, pipes, quotes) is written as `sh \-c '...'`, since mise quotes each word of a task's command.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet task apply [OPTIONS] <ID>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-file\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
Which file the user chose: local (mise.local.toml, theirs, not committed) or shared (mise.toml, committed)
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
Name the task NAME instead of the proposed name
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what mise would write, writing nothing
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<ID>\fR
The proposal, such as t2
.SH "JOUET SHARE"
Propose a proven rule, a missing primitive, or a fix to jouet itself to its maintainers

Builds a proposal and prints the exact text, with paths, repository names, hostnames and emails replaced, and a diff of what that changed. Nothing is posted without \-\-yes, which a person must approve: it posts with your GitHub login, a bug as an Issue on jdx/jouet and anything else as a Discussion in its "Rules & ideas" category. A rule is shared only once it passes its tests, has denied for a week and fired often with few overrides. \-\-idea also covers what sessions show about jouet itself: crashes, exit codes outside its table, malformed JSON, usage errors, output agents abandon, built\-in rules overridden again and again, slow hooks, and scripts written in many sessions. At most three posts a week.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet share [OPTIONS] [<NAME>]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-idea\fR
Share an idea: a missing primitive, or something sessions show about jouet itself
.TP
\fB\-\-list\fR
List the ideas ready to share, with their keys
.TP
\fB\-\-yes\fR
Post it; without this, only show what would be posted
.TP
\fB\-\-redact\fR \fI<WORD>\fR
Hide WORD as well, such as an internal tool's name. Repeatable
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<NAME>\fR
The rule to share; with \-\-idea, the idea's key (default: the most recorded)
.SH "JOUET AGENT-HELP"
Print a short usage guide written for agents

When to reach for each command, its key flags, the exit codes and the output modes, generated from this binary's own CLI definition. With a command, prints the invocations agents use, each with what it does, and what \-\-full adds: every subcommand and flag, and the details. With \-\-json, `text` is always the whole guide and `usage` the short one.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet agent\-help [OPTIONS] [<COMMAND>] ...
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-full\fR
Print the command's whole guide: every subcommand and flag, and the details
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<COMMAND>\fR
A command to describe, such as `edit`
.SH "JOUET SKILL"
Print jouet's agent skill

A short skill that says when to reach for jouet and points at `jouet agent\-help` for the flags. `jouet setup` installs it.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet skill [OPTIONS]
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fI<FORMAT>\fR
claude or codex (a SKILL.md), or agents\-md (a snippet for AGENTS.md)
.TP
\fB\-\-contribute\fR
Print the optional skill for proposing rules and ideas to jouet instead
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "JOUET SETUP"
Install jouet's hooks into a coding agent's harness

Merges hooks into Claude Code's settings.json, Codex's hooks.json, Cursor's hooks.json, Gemini CLI's settings.json or Copilot's hooks/jouet.json, keeping every other hook. The session\-start hook primes each session with the commands and rules, so the skill, which says the same, is installed only with \-\-skill or when priming is off ([hooks] prime). Running it again changes nothing; \-\-uninstall removes only what jouet added. The hooks do nothing where jouet is not on PATH.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet setup [OPTIONS] <HARNESS>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-global\fR
Install for every project, under your home directory (the default)
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR
Install into this repository for you alone: Claude Code's untracked .claude/settings.local.json
.TP
\fB\-\-shared\fR
Install into the repository's tracked hooks file (.claude/settings.json, .codex/hooks.json, .cursor/hooks.json, .gemini/settings.json or .github/hooks/jouet.json), for everyone who clones it (implies \-\-project)
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would change without writing anything
.TP
\fB\-\-uninstall\fR
Remove jouet's hooks and skill
.TP
\fB\-\-skill\fR
Install the skill as well, which the session\-start primer otherwise stands in for
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<HARNESS>\fR
claude, codex, cursor, gemini or copilot
.SH "JOUET COMPLETION"
Print a shell completion script

The script asks this binary for each completion, so it needs nothing else installed and follows whichever jouet is on PATH. Write it where your shell loads completions, or source it from your shell's startup file.
.PP
\fBUsage:\fR jouet completion [OPTIONS] <SHELL>
.PP
\fBOptions:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB\-\-joue\fR
Complete `joue` instead, the name routines run under
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
Print help
\fBArguments:\fR
.PP
.TP
\fB<SHELL>\fR
The shell to complete for
